The pattern "reset local state when prop changes" is legitimately useful here (when the server returns a new myScore after refresh, or when navigating between tracks). The React-recommended approach that avoids the effect is to compare against the previous prop during render and adjust state. React 19 supports this pattern cleanly with `useState` + a "previous" tracking during render:

```tsx
const [selected, setSelected] = useState<number | null>(myScore);
const [prevMyScore, setPrevMyScore] = useState<number | null>(myScore);

if (prevMyScore !== myScore) {
  setPrevMyScore(myScore);
  setSelected(myScore);
}
```

This is the official React docs pattern for "Adjusting state when a prop changes". However, the react-hooks lint rule might still complain? The rule `react-hooks/set-state-in-effect` specifically targets effects. Setting state during render is a different pattern and the compiler handles it. Actually with React Compiler lint, setting state during render of the same component is allowed (it's the documented "derived state" pattern). Let me use that.

Actually, wait — there's a subtlety. The rule `set-state-in-render` exists as its own rule? The React hooks ESLint plugin has `react-hooks/set-state-in-render`? Hmm, I recall rules: `react-hooks/set-state-in-effect` (new). There's also a rule about setting state in render... Let me check. In eslint-plugin-react-hooks v6, there's `react-hooks/set-state-in-render`? I'm not 100% sure. The docs pattern for adjusting state when props change is exactly the render-time comparison. React docs explicitly sanction it. But the lint might flag `setState` in render too if there's such a rule and it doesn't recognize the guard.

Alternative simpler approach that avoids the lint entirely: don't keep state in sync with prop via effect. Instead, use the "key" approach at the parent — but VoteControl is rendered inside TrackCard, prop myScore comes from server. 

Another clean approach: drop `selected` local state and drive everything from `myScore` prop + a transient "pending vote" state. Optimistic UI: while pending, show the optimistic score; on completion, router.refresh() updates myScore prop. The issue: if result not ok, revert to myScore (already handled by prop). And there's no need to resync from prop changes because the prop IS the source of truth.

Let me refactor:
```tsx
const [optimistic, setOptimistic] = useState<number | null>(null);
const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null);
const [pending, setPending] = useState(false);

const selected = optimistic ?? myScore;

async function vote(score) {
  if (pending || score === selected) return;
  setPending(true);
  setError(null);
  setOptimistic(score);
  const result = await setVote(trackId, score);
  setPending(false);
  if (!result.ok) {
    setError(result.error);
    setOptimistic(null);
    return;
  }
  router.refresh();
}
```

This is cleaner: no effect at all. `myScore` prop is source of truth; `optimistic` overrides while a vote is in flight; on refresh the new myScore arrives and we clear optimistic (set it to null after router.refresh). Actually after router.refresh, the component re-renders with new myScore; we should clear optimistic then. Setting setOptimistic(null) before router.refresh()... but then selected falls back to old myScore until refresh completes → visual flicker. Hmm.

Better: keep optimistic until new render. But when does it reset? If refresh returns same score, myScore prop = new value; optimistic still holds old pending value which equals new myScore anyway (the optimistic value IS the new score). So leaving optimistic set doesn't hurt: selected = optimistic = new score = myScore. Consistent.

Edge: on error we clear optimistic. Good.

But there's a subtle issue: if myScore prop changes externally (e.g., server returns different value than what we optimistically set — can't happen here since we set exactly what the server will store). Fine.

This removes the effect entirely and satisfies the linter. Let me rewrite VoteControl accordingly. Remove `useEffect` import.

Wait, there's still one consideration: initial `selected` shows `myScore` (e.g., 7). After refresh, myScore=8 → optimistic still 8 (from previous vote) → selected 8. Consistent. If user votes again to 5 → optimistic=5, selected=5. Consistent. Good.

Also `reached` logic unchanged.

Now the lib/auth.ts warning: line 18 `// eslint-disable-next-line no-var` is unused because `no-var` isn't enabled or the rule doesn't fire. Remove that comment. Also the `declare global` + eslint-disable — the disable-next-line applies to the `var` declaration line (line 19). Since it's unused, remove it.

Let me make both edits.

